{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Phase 1 RCA for the third organic recurrence — confirmed test-harness defect.\n\nThe new signature is not the prior missing-loose-blob class. In a real fixture repo, deleting the exact 1005 loose blob (66842fd006b2fdc95ff55a33a263f1cb216bc7d6) left BOTH the directory-wide git log and the immediate per-event git log healthy: broad rc=0/stdout=804 bytes; narrow rc=0/one SHA. Therefore the earlier ENOENT mechanism cannot produce this batched-empty/narrow-success recurrence.\n\nThe changeable defect is the equivalence test at tests/unit/test_authorship_batched_ticket_map_7084.py:184 treating one raw call to build_ticket_position_commit_map as guaranteed non-empty even though the function contract at src/rebar/attest/authorship.py:294-378 explicitly returns {} on any Git/process/empty-output failure and production callers fall back per event. The repository already records that Git reads can transiently return rc!=0 or empty stdout under CI load and require bounded recovery (tests/_git_counts.py:1-55). The CI observation is exactly one empty batched result followed immediately by a successful per-event read in the same repo.\n\nDiscriminating runtime injection reproduced the complete observed sequence for every single-attempt transient boundary: first rc=128/empty stderr payload, rc=0 with empty stdout, and BlockingIOError(EAGAIN) each made the raw batched helper return {}; an immediate replay of identical argv against unchanged state returned all 8 positions (rc=0, 804-byte stdout). Three other CI lanes and the local fixture use the same deterministic topology successfully, ruling out a stable parser/topology defect. The lower-level transient class is intentionally fail-closed and may be rc, empty output, or process-spawn pressure; the test bug is requiring that optimization-only call to succeed on its first environmental attempt instead of using bounded recovery while retaining a loud persistent-failure diagnostic.\n\nDefect legitimacy: defect in the test harness, not production authorship behavior. The production contract preserves attribution via per-event fallback; the test should isolate the equivalence property from a documented transient Git-read boundary. Phase 2 will be test-only: add bounded exact-call recovery for fixture-backed batched-map assertions, inject the first-attempt transient as the RED oracle, preserve persistent-failure diagnostics, and leave src/rebar unchanged."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786905357129197001,"uuid":"161c9e16-26fb-45e6-aa7e-56000ce32e04"}